Logical bug in reviewer subpart mark assignment. `previous` can be `null` or `0` (both are falsy in JavaScript). The check `if (!previous)` will evaluate to `true` for both `null` and `0`, causing the script to overwrite a valid mark of `0` or bypass the `else if` block entirely. It should explicitly check for `undefined`.

```javascript
    const previous = paths.get(event.label);
    if (previous === undefined) paths.set(event.label, marks.at(-1) ?? null);
    else if (previous === null && marks.length) paths.set(event.label, marks.at(-1));
```

